+**Formulas**
+The total distortion loss is defined as the sum of two terms [S1][S2, unofficial]:
 
-**Pseudocode:**
+$$
+\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}(\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{w}) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}} + \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{2nd}}
+$$
+
+1.  **First Term (Pairwise Midpoint Distances):**
+    $$
+    \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}} = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} w_i w_j |m_i - m_j|
+    $$
+    This term is minimized by pulling distant intervals towards each other and consolidating weight into a single interval or a small number of nearby intervals [S1].
+
+2.  **Second Term (Interval Sizes):**
+    $$
+    \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{2nd}} = \frac{1}{3} \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} w_i^2 (s_{i+1} - s_i)
+    $$
+    This term minimizes the weighted size of each individual interval, encouraging compactness [S1].
+
+**Efficient O(N) Computation**
+The naive double summation in $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}}$ is $O(N^2)$. To achieve $O(N)$, we eliminate the diagonal term ($i=j$) and exploit the ordering $m_i < m_{i+1}$ [S2, unofficial]:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}} &= \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} w_i w_j |m_i - m_j| \\
+&= 2 \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} w_i w_j (m_i - m_j) \\
+&= 2 \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} w_i m_i \left( \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} w_j \right) - 2 \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} w_i \left( \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} w_j m_j \right)
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+Let $P_w[i] = \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} w_j$ and $P_{wm}[i] = \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} w_j m_j$. These prefix sums can be computed in $O(N)$. The loss is then:
+$$
+\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}} = 2 \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} w_i m_i P_w[i] - 2 \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} w_i P_{wm}[i]
+$$
+
+**Gradients**
+The gradient with respect to weight $w_k$ for the first term is derived as [S2, unofficial]:
+
+$$
+\frac{\partial}{\partial w_k} \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{1st}} = 2m_k \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} w_j - 2 \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} w_j m_j + 2 \sum_{i=k+1}^{N-1} w_i m_i - 2m_k \sum_{i=k+1}^{N-1} w_i
+$$
+
+This can be computed in $O(1)$ per $k$ using precomputed prefix sums ($P_w, P_{wm}$) and suffix sums ($S_w, S_{wm}$), where $S_w[k] = \sum_{i=k+1}^{N-1} w_i$ and $S_{wm}[k] = \sum_{i=k+1}^{N-1} w_i m_i$.
+
+The gradient for the second term is:
+$$
+\frac{\partial}{\partial w_k} \mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}^{\text{2nd}} = \frac{2}{3} w_k (s_{k+1} - s_k)
+$$
+
